// @run
// @log 1
// @log 2
// @log 1
// @log q
// @log 1
// @log 1.5
// @log true
// @log 4
// A generic alias whose type-PARAMETER name shadows a builtin binds NOTHING, so its
// APPLICATION must substitute nothing: `type Box<i32> = { v: i32 }` has a field of type
// `i32`, and `Box<string>` is `{v: i32}`, not `{v: string}`.
//
// This is the checker's leaf-resolution ORDER, not a taste: `tsLeafTy` asks
// `primTyOfName` FIRST and the live parameter binding second, so a primitive spelling
// wins its own name. `emit_base.gaeParamShadowed` is that order read as a predicate, and
// `gaeApplyFieldTy` consults it before substituting at ANY depth. Without it the emitter
// rewrites the field to the ARGUMENT while the checker keeps the BUILTIN — two answers
// for one field, `vl check` rc=0, and a module that does not even PARSE
// ("expected (ref $type), found i32").
//
// Held fixed while varying: one alias, one field, one application at a type OTHER than
// the shadowed one. What moves is the shadowed primitive (i32 / string / f64 / boolean)
// and the depth the field writes it at (`i32`, `i32[]`, `i32[][]`) — the ladder that
// substitutes must decline at every rung it reaches, not just the first.
//
// `boolean` is here because it is the one primitive that CANNOT witness the defect on
// its own: it shares i32's rep, so a `boolean`↔`i32` swap emits valid (if wrong) code.
// It is pinned as the rep-blind cell, and the non-i32-rep cells beside it (string, f64)
// are the ones that catch a regression. The identity application `Box<i32>` closes the
// set from the other side: substituting there is a no-op, so it must keep running.
//
// `type-param-name-shadows-builtin.vl` is the DECLARATION-side sibling (the arm that
// keeps `collectA` forcing a genuine `i32[]`'s heap types), and
// `type-param-shadows-declared-struct.vl` is this guard's inverted control — a parameter
// named for a DECLARED type is a real parameter and must still substitute.
type BoxI<i32> = { v: i32 }

type BoxS<string> = { v: string }

type BoxF<f64> = { v: f64 }

type BoxB<boolean> = { v: boolean }

type ArrI<i32> = { v: i32[] }

type NestI<i32> = { v: i32[][] }

const a: BoxI<string> = { v: 1 }
print(a.v)

const b: ArrI<string> = { v: [1, 2] }
print(b.v.length)
print(b.v[0])

const c: BoxS<i32> = { v: "q" }
print(c.v)

const d: NestI<string> = { v: [[1, 2]] }
print(d.v.length)

const e: BoxF<string> = { v: 1.5 }
print(e.v)

const f: BoxB<string> = { v: true }
print(f.v)

const g: BoxI<i32> = { v: 4 }
print(g.v)
